speakersafetyd is a software "smart amp": it protects the loudspeakers of
Apple Silicon Macs from thermal damage.

The amplifiers in these machines report the voltage across and the current
through each speaker's voice coil, but do nothing with it themselves; macOS
does the protection in userland.  speakersafetyd reads those sense signals,
estimates the voice coil and magnet temperatures with a two-stage thermal
model, and turns the speaker volume down when they approach their limit.
This lets the speakers be driven well above the level that would be safe
for the worst possible input.

The per-machine thermal parameters for the supported models are included.
The daemon needs kernel drivers that expose the sense signals and a
volume interlock; it does nothing useful without them.
